TI  - CTFN: Multi-scale CNN and transformer with graph encodings fusion network
      for hyperspectral image classification
T2  - Expert Syst. Appl.
VL  - 288
IS  - C
PY  - 2025
DA  - 2025/9
PB  - Pergamon Press, Inc.
CY  - USA
SN  - 0957-4174
DO  - 10.1016/j.eswa.2025.128063
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2025.128063
UR  - http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.eswa.2025.128063
KW  - Hyperspectral image classification, Convolution neural network, Transformer, Graph convolution network
AB  - Due to the powerful feature representation capabilities, deep learning methods have been widely applied to hyperspectral image (HSI) classification in recent years. Among these methods, the convolutional neural network (CNN) can effectively represent the spatial contextual relationships of HSI, while the Transformer can establish long-range dependencies between different land covers. They have been proven to be powerful feature extractors in HSI classification. However, due to the geometric constraints of the convolution kernel, CNN has difficulty in mining long-distance spatial information. The Transformer is prone to ignoring local information. To solve these problems, this paper proposes a Multi-Scale Spectral Domain Attention Convolution and Graph Encoding Transformation Fusion Network (CTFN) that more effectively exploits spatial-spectral information. Specifically, we develop multi-scale convolution with spectral self-attention (MCSA) to comprehensively capture spatial-contextual information through multi-scale convolutional kernels, while integrating a spectral self-attention mechanism for extracting discriminative spectral features. In addition, we introduce graph encodings in the Transformer, allowing the Transformer to characterize the graph structure while paying attention to local spatial information. Finally, we propose a pixel-superpixel fusion module to extract coarse and fine-grained features from HSI and obtain joint outputs. We verify the effectiveness of the proposed algorithm on three standard datasets, and CTFN achieves optimal performance compared with the current state-of-the-art classification frameworks. The CTFN code will be released at https://github.com/ahappyyang/CTFN.
ER  -

